ink if I  had to choose one factor it would it  would be a letter that I had uncovered I  had discovered this letter written by a  local agent a plantation agent we  sometime refer to them as attorneys who  were acting on behalf of absentee  employers or absentee owners of the  plantations who lived in England  and the tradition within Jamaican slave  societies that those local attorneys  would write letters to employers in  England detail in and given reports  about what was going on in the colony  the ways in which they were managing the  plantations the ways in which they were  treating the enslaved people and so I  found this letter written somewhere  around 1804 from this local agent in  Jamaica to his employer in England and  in this letter he detailed the ways in  which he was changing around the labour  responsibilities of enslaved women so in  one part of the letter he explained that  enslaved women who were pregnant would  not work on their own they would work in  groups of three and by placing them in  groups of three to say lift canes to the  mill this would allow some women to rest  while others work so he was punctuating  their labour with rest period and when I  saw that it struck and note with me and  he struck a note with me because I sort  of read it through the lens of several  historian so Jennifer Morgan Kathy Brown  Vereen Shepherd they all have sort of  written about history of slavery in the  body and just you know the body in  general and the ways in which you know  the body sort of isn't just this thing  that we write about it is this thing  that forces us to respond to it in  certain ways and when I saw that letter  that talked about the ways in which this  local agent was responding to a woman  being pregnant and change in the labour  roles to respond to that pregnancy it  got me thinking about you know what  these other his stories had written  about the body of enslaved people and  what that actually meant in the context  of slavery and