 slavery really as horrible as these  abolitionists were charging so you had a  ship servant surgeon sorry  sailors captains traders doctors  planters so just about everyone who had  any kind of dealings with either the  slave trade or slavery appeared before  Parliament to answer questions and so  one of these essential sources that I've  had to rely on and you know one of the  sources that gives me quite a bit of  detail on these women are actually these  parliamentary records you know they  asked a lot of question about you know  what kind of labor were pregnant women  subjected to what kinds of punishment  were  any specific kind of allowances given to  them in terms of increased food rations  in terms of increased clothing  allowances and so these questions and  the responses became a very rich set of  sources but of course all these turns  again will tell you that their sources  are inherently biased and the kind of  bias that I was working with with these  sources is these planters obviously are  in trouble or in their in some other  trouble they have to prove to the  British Parliament that  oh those abolitionists are you know  making up story there they're not  telling the truth they're really making  on phone charges and so the potato  particular kind of bias within these  sources is that the planters would  exaggerate the extent of their  benevolence they would exaggerate that  oh yes we're actually you know making  life a lot more bearable for these women  and so yes on the one hand these  slaveholders did in fact make some  changes to women's our lives and women's  oven conditions but I had to figure out  how truthful were they being you know  how extensive were these changes and so  I had to counterbalance out those  witness testimonies by using other  sources so some of the sources that I  would look at were the private  correspondence so that same letter that  I talked about that got me involved in  this project was one of the you know one  of the kind of sources that I use so  private correspondence between local  agents in Jamaica who were right into  employers in England as well as if you  made more objective sources so these are  plantation inventories that would list  the number of enslaved people on a  particular plantation it would list the  number of children who were born at a  given time the number of individuals who  died it also lists the general work log  so you know how many and say people are  on a property what are the work that  they're involved in doing and so it's  painstaking research because you sort of  want to get as close as possible to the  reality of these women's lives and one  of the other sort of challenge that I  had to deal with is none of these  sources were actually written by the  enslaved women right so it's all these  slave holders up  parliamentarians their perspective that  were a first-person nothing in your eye  that's right nothing in the first-person  from these enslave people's perspective  and so what I had to do in the book is  to put it at the front and center to say  well you know these are the perspectives  of the enslaved people and this is my  best guess and what they might be  thinking what they might be getting at  in terms of how they're responding to  the slaveholders and so it's really you  know painstaking archival dig that I had  to go through and get in this work did  you come across any women's voices at  all I clearly obviously not women who  were enslaved but any you know the wife  of a plantation owner or any other kind  of woman's voice was heard in the  researcher it was a predominantly male  so yes and no it was predominantly white  men's voices there are some white women  that I haven't covered so planters wives  the governor's wife the the governor in  Jamaica from 1803 - I think 1804 his  wife sort of you know has this journal  that a lot of us historians have relied  on so there are occasional our records  from white women that I've been able to  access as well and the interesting thing  is that it's very difficult at times to  tell differently the perspective of a  white male planter from the perspective  of a white woman of his wife because  their economic interests in the  plantation in slavery their ideological  support of slavery were pretty much  identical so it was really very  difficult to disentangle a white woman's  perspective from a white males  perspective in terms of enslaved  people's perspective unlike the American  experience where for example they've  been able to rely on the WPA narratives  we don't sort of have a similar set of  sources for the Caribbean though we have  for example the narrative of an enslaved  woman named Mary prince whose owners had  taken her to England and then she was  able to escape to abolitionists and then  tell her story to these abolitionists so  there are these occasional sources that  were able to rely on  I'm very careful in my work not to sort  of use one person's story to tell  everyone else's story so even though  there are times when I pull on mayor  princes experience I try not to use that  to generalize and say well mayor friends  thought bass mayor friends experienced  that and so it must be true for either  and say woman I try as much as possible  to think of these women as having very  diverse experiences  okay let's you know it strikes me when  you were talking about the the first  report that went back to England talking  about how he the slave owner or whoever  was managing the plantation grouped  these pregnant woman together and it's  and my immediate thought was it seems  like he would have had to do that if he  wanted them to continue working because  depending on where they are on their  pregnancy how much work in a pregnant  woman really do of hard physical labor  my guess would be not a lot and it you  know it's very difficult for the women  so my next thought was what did you come  across in your research any means that  plantation owners or slave owners took  to not have the women slaves get  pregnant because it would have because  it probably slows down on production  yeah and also cost them extra in food  and clothing as you had pointed out  that's right that's right  so that is an interesting question and  that sort of the crooks of the book and  I sort of go through and explain that  the 17:8 is to 1834 which is a time  period that my work sort of senators on  is a moment of change it's a moment of  change for several reasons one of which  is before the 1780s before abolitionists  came up with this elaborate plan to end  the slave trade by way of getting these  women to reproduce the population  locally planters really were not as  invested in reproduction and they were  not as invested for several reasons one  they calculated as you've said that you  know it was probably too expensive  because they would have had to provide  for some kind of allowance for women  during pregnancy they would have had to  have extra rations for children and even  these children who were born it would  have been at least several years before  they would be viable laborers and so a  lot of planters before the 1780s were  not as willing to invest in caring for  pregnant women in caring for enslaved  children and in some respects as I had  mentioned earlier this is partly why  historian so said that the plantation  regime was gender blind because planters  were not as attentive to differences as  it pertains to sexual differences as it  pertains to the different needs of  childbearing women and so there is a  degree of accuracy in making that  argument but this changes in the 1780s  and the change is that abolitionist  agitation interests by the British  government and of course by 1807 when  the slave trade does in fact comes to an  end  planters quickly realized that the kind  of neglect and inattention that they had  given to childbearing women before the  1780s that could no longer continue and  so now was a real moment for them to  take stock of how they had treated and  slave women in terms of the kind of  labor roles that they were given in  terms of how they would be punished and  so this is sort of a real moment of  change that were seeing in terms of you  know and save women's experiences on  these plantations okay let's talk about  some of the conflicts that you had  mentioned earlier just give a couple of  specific examples if you would please  so that's dangerous when you ask  historians for example his thorns can  talk forever I think one of my favorite  conflicts that I talk about in the book  is a conflict between doctors and and  slave midwives healers and enslave women  so one of the things that stakeholders  started to do in the 1780s is they  started employing doctors and these are  white European doctors who are coming  out of England mostly and in some cases  Scotland to the colony to Jamaica and  they hire them for a number of reasons  they hire them to  ten specifically to pregnant women but  they also hire them to attend to the  general health care off the plantation  but the interesting thing is that before  this period in the 1780s there might  have been one or two doctors in the  Caribbean most of the health care  practices were actually performed by  enslaved healers and so when these uh  white doctors came out to Jamaica in the  1780s and especially by the 1800s now  seeking to take over health care on the  plantations the inside healer said wait  a minute you know this is my territory  this is sort of my domain and so they  didn't simply just roll over and allow  white doctors to manage childbirth and  to manage healthcare these women sort of  resisted the idea of having now these  practices under the control of white  doctors but even beyond that these and  slave women who were pregnant would not  willingly give over the care of their  bodies to these white doctors remember  that this is very much a racialized  social order and so whiteness sort of  came with this so the white doctors were  seen as allies of the planters and so  enslaved women didn't trust those  doctors so even though at times there  were conflicts I do talk about conflicts  between doctors and planters who had  very different ideas about how to treat  trial childbearing woman the enslaved  woman didn't trust these doctors either  and so they one refused to be treated by  them and then the the healers and  midwives who were part of the plantation  system before the coming of these  doctors also resisted these doctors  coming in and essentially taking over so  I'd say sort of that might be my  favorite conflict and to think of a  specific example they had these debates  about whether or not infants should be  bathed and if infants are allowed to be  bathed should they be bathed in hot  water or should they be bathed in cold  water and in slave women insisted that  you know infants should have bath you  should bathe the infant because you know  bathing them was an essential birth  ritual and they would use warm water and  the water warm water would be infused  with rum and the doctors had a very  different idea first and foremost  Europeans had a long and checkered  history with faith and they had  superstitious ideas that bathing would  sort of cause the body to deplete either  it's worth it would cause it to lose its  humoral balance and so they had these  prescriptions to say oh you shouldn't  bathe and if you did in fact take a bath  only certain areas of the body should be  washed and so there was this clash  between and slave healers and then slave  midwives with your European doctors who  are coming one with very different ideas  about bathing and also different ideas  about bathing infants and so one of the  biggest crash that I talk about in my  book is about the approach to bathe in  both the pregnant women as well as  enslaved children so I think that might  be one of my favorite examples of you  know the kind of conflict that is taking  place on the ground with enslaved women  and these European doctors who are  coming into Jamaica so what do you  ultimately conclude any of book so I  make several conclusions one of the  conclusions that I make and I think I  started by talking about the  abolitionists one of the conclusions as  it pertains to that aspect of my book is  that the abolitionists did not have the  freedom and equality of enslaved people  in mind it wasn't their central  objective the central objective of  abolitionists was to protect British  imperial interests British colonial  interests and the reason I make that  argument is that if abolitionists were  first and foremost concerned with  freedom and equality for enslaved people  then the moral outrage that they had  over slavery that would be sufficient  for them to say it doesn't matter if  this slave traders will lose the money  it doesn't matter what the economic  consequences of that loss might actually  be the slave trade is so repugnant the  slave trade is so inhumane that we  require that the slave trade to end  immediately instead what they did was to  create this very elaborate scheme to  continue the slave trade for ten to  fifteen years and to have women  reproduce the population locally and so  what I do is sort of you know make this  argument that abolition was first and  foremost about securing British colonial  interests and less so about equality and  freedom for the enslaved population the  other conclusion that I make is that  this period is often labeled as a period  of amelioration by historians so they  call the period between 1780s 1834  one of amelioration and we debate  whether or not the condition of the  ensei person had improved so you know  did as they read get better in its  silent years and I sort of shift away  from that argument and I shift away from  it because even where you might be able  to see where and say women for example  had greater access to food or had you  know a less demanding workload what  changed here in the period 1780 to 1834  is that there were new demands being  placed on enslaved women so the only  reason that planters gave them more food  or gave them more clothing ration or  mitigated their punishment was to be  able to harness their reproductive  potential and so for me it's difficult  to sort of get into this binary of  better or worse but to say that actually  we're seeing the very parameters upon  which slavery here was being built are  actually changing during this period so  we're focused on the wrong thing to say  you know that slavery got better in the  end of years so I think those are the  two major conclusions that I make in the  book well this has been fascinating  thank you so much for being here with us  today and sharing your work thank you  thank you for having me  for more information about professor  Turner and her research please visit our  website at macmillan report yale.edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at new  you
 

